<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Birkenfeld</h1><p class="st-meta">1817 CE – 1937 CE · capital Birkenfeld</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hunsrück exclave of the Grand Duchy of Oldenburg, ruled from the North Sea coast for more than a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, the Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> was awarded territory on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. In 1817 he received a district of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarre-department">French Saar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkenfeld">Birkenfeld</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a>, which became the Principality of Birkenfeld, an exclave hundreds of kilometres from Oldenburg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was governed by a regional government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkenfeld">Birkenfeld</a> under Oldenburg's laws and had its own provincial council. Its people were mostly Protestant farmers, with gemstone cutting and agate working around Idar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idar-oberstein">Oberstein</a> as a distinctive industry. After 1918 it formed a part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-oldenburg">Free State of Oldenburg</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-hamburg-act">Greater Hamburg Act</a> of 1937, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkenfeld">Birkenfeld</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, which made it a district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>. It is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-birkenfeld.jpg" alt="Principality of Birkenfeld" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Richard Andree · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuerstentum_Birkenfeld.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1817 CE – 1937 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Birkenfeld</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Peter Frederick Louis, Frederick Augustus II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstentum Birkenfeld</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1877&amp;focus=principality-of-birkenfeld" aria-label="Principality of Birkenfeld on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4845 448 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Birkenfeld lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5165 704L5183 604M5165 704L5183 604M5165 704L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5183 604h0M5183 604h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5165 704h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5165 704h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Birkenfeld. <a href="/atlas?year=1877&amp;focus=principality-of-birkenfeld">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
